\section{Measuring Cdr Locality on a Retained Heap Graph}

To reproduce the shape of Clark and Green's locality question
without their Interlisp-10 images, record a stopped heap graph
and walk it from a fixed list of roots. Each reachable pair has
an address and two field values; fields can be atoms or pair
pointers. The snapshot uses fixed-size pair slots and a
specified page size in slots. Count pair-to-pair cdr edges
and the fraction whose source and target share a page. Visit
both fields to discover the graph even though only cdr edges
enter the statistic. This measures static retained layout, not
the frequency or latency of cdr reads during execution.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input snapshot $H$, roots $R$, page size $p>0$ in slots
  \Output total pair cdr edges $n$, same-page edges $k$
  \State $Q\gets$ root pointers; $V\gets\varnothing$
  \State $(n,k)\gets(0,0)$
  \While{$Q$ is nonempty}
    \State $x\gets\Call{Remove}{Q}$
    \If{$x\notin V$}
      \State insert $x$ in $V$ \Comment{Count each source once}
      \State $(a,b)\gets H[x]$
      \State enqueue pair pointers among $a,b$ in $Q$
      \If{$b$ is a pair pointer}
        \State $n\gets n+1$
        \State add $1$ to $k$ if $\lfloor x/p\rfloor=\lfloor b/p\rfloor$
        \Comment{Use addresses from this snapshot}
      \EndIf
    \EndIf
  \EndWhile
  \State \Return $(n,k)$
\end{sudocode}

With four slots per page, let the root at slot $0$ point
through its car to slot $3$ and through its cdr to slot $1$.
Let the cdrs of $1$ and $3$ both point to slot $4$;
the cdr of $4$ is empty and all other fields are atoms.
The observed cdr edges are $0\to1$, $1\to4$, and
$3\to4$. Only the first stays on its page: report $1/3$.
In a controlled relocation, move slot $4$ to slot $2$ and
rewrite both pointers. The graph has the same sharing and
three edges, but all are now on the same page; report $3/3$.
An unrelated unreachable pair at slot $6$ contributes to
neither result.

\Cref{fig:visual-158} groups addresses by page. It distinguishes
the edges that discover pairs from the edges that enter the statistic.

